            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Gnashing of teeth primarily symbolizes the extreme anguish, despair, and remorse of the wicked suffering eschatological punishment in hell.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Gnashing of teeth symbolizes the hostile rage and hatred of the wicked directed at the righteous, particularly upon seeing themselves excluded from the kingdom.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Anguish, sorrow, and painful remorse of the damned under divine judgment.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Defiant rage and hatred of the wicked toward the righteous.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The primary consensus restricts the core meaning to eschatological punishment in the Gospels, while the book broadens the extension to include historical and earthly instances of hostility, such as in the Psalms and Acts.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book shifts the emotional core of the symbol from the suffering and painful remorse of eternal torment to a defiant, hostile rage directed at the righteous.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book explicitly contrasts its identification ('rage of the wicked') with the commonly taught view ('anguish of the damned'), drawing on Old Testament and Acts references to redefine the emotional reality of the Synoptic idiom.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In the Synoptic Gospels, 'gnashing of teeth' is consistently paired with 'weeping' in contexts of definitive divine punishment (e.g., the fiery furnace, outer darkness), which strongly suggests an idiom for profound sorrow, regret, and suffering rather than solely defiant rage.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
